From 649ad3767668974799f115eb5393dd3303809d26 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MasherDon <62326358+MasherDon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 11:49:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 48/92] moe-stream : the mailbox's reply (SiegeLadder G22), on
 by default - with the router's mailbox the remap's CPU split had no card
 input, but its answers (the slot ids for the expert GEMMs, the drop rule's or
 runahead's multipliers) still went to the card as inputs of the next card
 split: the scheduler synchronized the compute stream - which waits on the
 slots' copy events, so the host slept until the copies landed - copied each
 answer synchronously and only then launched the split, ~90 us of idle card
 per layer (SiegeLadder runs.md 4.76, bench/nsys_gaps.py). Now an op on the
 remap's split (llama_moe_stream_reply_0 / _1) copies the answers into the
 layer's reply record (two per layer, 512 bytes, after the mailbox records)
 with the number of the post they answer, and a new op, GGML_OP_MBOX_READ
 (CUDA: one block checks the reply's number against the post record's sequence
 word and copies the record into a card tensor; a mismatch gives skip ids
 0x7FFFFFFF / zero multipliers and writes the error word, which the next
 mailbox wait turns into an abort; a CPU version too), gives the card its own
 copy at the head of the next split: that split has no host input, the host
 launches it right after the remap while the copies are in flight, and the
 card starts the GEMMs on the copy events. Every mailbox path: the drop
 default, the exact path without runahead, both halves of runahead, A'.
 GGML_SCHED_DEBUG: splits with inputs 43 -> 8 of 86 per decode graph (the rest
 are the graph's own inputs at the token's start). SiegeLadder runs.md 4.77:
 text = trace, the deterministic counters equal to the unit, the deterministic
 mask mode (DROP_W=0.12 DROP_VRAM_ONLY=3) PPL / KL / 92 633 drops identical;
 exact runahead 13.63 -> 14.38 tok/s (+5.5%), the drop default 14.38 -> 14.88
 (+2...+3.5%), runahead off level; the card's hand-off idle 4.2 -> 1.9 ms per
 token, of which ~1 ms is now the card's wait for the copy events (32 us per
 layer). LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_MBOX_REPLY=0 - the scheduler's copy; off with
 LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_MASK_LATE_SPLIT. Also NVTX ranges for llama_decode's phases
 (decode, graph_build, graph_alloc, set_inputs, graph_compute;
 llama_moe_stream_nvtx_push / _pop): at a token's boundary the card waits 3.6
 ms, of which set_inputs 1.5, the graph build 0.95 and the allocation 0.7
 (SiegeLadder G23)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
